okay in this lesson we're going to talk  about square roots and radicals so this  is something that the makers of the act  obviously wanted us to understand  so let's talk about how to work with the  square roots and the radicals and then  we'll go through some  typical type examples that would be uh  on the test so the first example that we  want to talk about is just you know  recognizing that when you square  something versus when you square root  something those are just opposites and  inverses of one another okay so they  just undo or reverse one another out so  let's just talk about how to simplify  some of these square roots and then  we'll get into the more typical type  problems that might be asked on the act  so the square root of 32 you want to  know how to simplify that and the way  you do that is you divide out perfect  squares so you say like what's something  that  i can divide out a square root of 32  that's a perfect square well 16 times 2  see 16 is a perfect square the square  root of 16  is 4 so this is 4 square root of 2. now  square root of 2 i can't divide out any  perfect squares from there so i'm just  going to leave that as it is so 4 square  root of 2 would be the simplified  uh version of square root of 32. now  square root of 50 what can you divide  out of that well you can see there's a  25 times 2 square root of 25 is 5 and  the square root of 2 again we can't  simplify that one  okay so now the this one here is a  little bit more challenging because  we've got some variables involved  but what you want to do here when you  multiply is you want to multiply the  quantities on the outside of the square  roots together and the quantities on the  inside of the square roots together and  then simplify from there so 4 times 8 is  32  the square root of let's see 12  okay x squared times x is x cubed y  times y cubed is y to the fourth okay  now what we want to do is we want to  simplify this radical here square root  of 12 you can simplify that into  square root of 4 times square root of 3  okay and then x cubed you can break that  down into x squared times x and then y  to the fourth that's already  a perfect  square so let's see square root of 4 is  2 so 2 times 32 that gives us 64.  square root of x squared that gives us  just x okay square root of y to the  fourth that's just going to be y squared  because y squared times y squared gives  you back y to the fourth  square root of x is not a perfect square  and square root of three is not a  perfect square so that stays underneath  the radical  and that's it you've got it right there  okay let's look at some typical  typical examples  if 2x squared minus 9 equals 41 then  what does x squared plus 7 equal so  pause the video see if you can do this  one on your own and then we'll go  through it  okay so what you want to do here there's  a couple different ways to do this  problem but i think the most common way  that students would do this is just go  ahead and solve for x then we can put x  back in here and simplify so what we're  going to do is we're going to add 9 to  both sides so that gives you 2x squared  equals 50.  okay then we're going to work from the  outside in towards that x by doing the  opposites we're going to divide both  sides by 2  okay so that's x squared equals 25 and  then instead of squaring we're going to  do the inverse we're going to take the  square root of both sides so x equals  plus or minus 5. okay when you take the  square root of both sides of the  equation get two answers positive or  negative because a negative squared is a  positive and a positive squared is a  positive  now what we're going to do is we're  going to take that plus or minus 5 and  put it back in here well whether it's  negative or positive when you square it  a negative times a negative is a  positive positive times a positive is a  positive so negative 5 or positive pi  squared that's going to give you 25  plus 7 equals  32. so the answer to this question is  going to be 32. okay now example number  two  see if you can do this one if the square  of a number is between  908  and 1447  which of the following could possibly be  the number and they give us a bunch of  choices here 18 29 33 39 okay or 41.  so see if you can do this one on your  own go ahead and pause the video and  we'll do it together  now the thing they want us to realize is  that the square of a number okay so  basically if you square the number  you're going to get  908  or if you square the number you can get  1 447 and somewhere in between okay  these two that's our number right so  what they're testing us on is you know  do we recognize that squaring and square  rooting are inverses of each other if we  go ahead and take the square root of  both sides let's see what we get okay  i'm going to go to the calculator and  just see what this comes out to so  square root of 908  is 30.1 approximately okay and the  square root of 1447  is about 38 okay  so we're somewhere in between 30 and 38  so which one would that be well it can't  be 18 or 29 33 is in between 39 is not  41 is not so it looks like the best  answer here is letter c  33. so again just understanding how to  work with radicals and simplifying as  well as you know understanding that  square roots and  squares are inverses of each other is  important for you to  understand for the a c t

